A||||8||DXR_DAD||Sensors, which one?||||5
Z||000000||DXR_DAD||11-11-1999||03:03 AM||||Just need to know, what is the differance?<BR>Manifold Absolute Pressure, and Mainfold Differential Pressure. Do they do the same job?||||reg||5
Z||000001||theogre||11-12-1999||01:27 PM||||If your asking what I think you are, yes they are the same.<P>A MAP sensor actually reads the diferance between atmospheric presure and manifold pressure. There is a hole on the MAP sensor someplace that lets outside air get into it. sometimes it's hard to find but it's there.||||reg||1
Z||000002||DXR_DAD||11-12-1999||07:31 PM||||Thanks ogre, thats what I wanted to know.||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||11-12-1999||10:54 PM||||Haynes has a book on EFI that while general does explain rather clearly what each sensor is and how it works. The books are broken into year ranges not make/model. If you get the one that covers 86-96 then you will get all the Fiero systems including the early 300 TBI and 700 TBI used 87 and up so that one book covers all fiero. (Haynes number 10220) I got a copy because it was the only book I could find that had a decent diagram of the 700 which isn't in the Fiero book.||||reg||1
Z||000004||DXR_DAD||11-12-1999||11:10 PM||||Thanks again ogre, I needed info on the 700, I just got a 87. I remember your post about sensors,and needing to replace them sooner or later, I did find some good info in Hot Rod Performance Series  "FUEL INJECTION BASICS" It does give good info on all types of EFI sensors. The MAP sensor is bad on my 87 I pulled a code 34, too late to check with  v-meter might just replace it anyway?  But again you come through. <P>"THE GREAT OGRE"<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000006||theogre||11-13-1999||10:10 PM||||Well..... I don't know if agree with all that,,, but anyway I reread my haynes description. The MAP sensor apparantly uses the atmospheric port for altitude corection. At least that's what haynes says. I know the port is there..... I have a Wells replacement MAP and you can easily see the port right on top of it. I can't see it on the OEM on, but that may just mean GM hid it better.<P>I can't find any decent documentation specific to GM's MAP. Nuts.||||reg||1
Z||000007||DXR_DAD||11-14-1999||01:22 AM||||Ogre, Chapter 6 page 158 of my Haynes<BR>Re: 2.5L as having Manifold differential pressure, 2.8L has Mainfold absolute pressure<P>Just for name sake they do the same thing. Every other reference in the book refer to "it"  as a MAP sensor??||||reg||1
Z||000008||theogre||11-14-1999||01:14 PM||||what ever you call it, and however it works, yes, the end result is the same. it tells the ECM that manifold vacuum has changed.||||reg||1
Z||000009||DXR_DAD||11-14-1999||10:54 PM||||Replaced that little bugger, YES!!! That was the problem my new "87" runs like a champ. The guy I got it from was fed up with having to do repairs all the time, the car is loaded with new parts including a new clutch. I feel much better about it.<P>Ogre I have been reading your old post on the DIS I will need some of this info NOW! Anything new please post it.  <P>Thanks||||reg||1
